  "id": "image-rotation-nearest",
  "title": "Image Rotation (Nearest Neighbor)",
  "description": "Rotate a two-dimensional image counterclockwise around its center using nearest-neighbor sampling. The output must have the same height and width as the input image.\n\nFor an image with H rows and W columns, define the center row coordinate:\n\n$$\nc_y = \\frac{H - 1}{2}\n$$\n\nDefine the center column coordinate:\n\n$$\nc_x = \\frac{W - 1}{2}\n$$\n\nLet a be the supplied angle in degrees. Convert it to radians:\n\n$$\n\\theta = a\\frac{\\pi}{180}\n$$\n\nFor each output position with row i and column j, subtract the image center to obtain dy = i - c_y and dx = j - c_x. Use inverse rotation to locate the corresponding source row:\n\n$$\ns_y = c_y + dy\\cos(\\theta) + dx\\sin(\\theta)\n$$\n\nCompute the corresponding source column:\n\n$$\ns_x = c_x - dy\\sin(\\theta) + dx\\cos(\\theta)\n$$\n\nRound s_y and s_x to the nearest integers using Python round. If both rounded coordinates are inside the input image, copy that source pixel. Otherwise, place 0 at the output position.\n\nHere, i and j are output coordinates, s_y and s_x are source coordinates, and theta is the counterclockwise rotation angle in radians.\n\nReturn the rotated image as a two-dimensional list with the same dimensions as the input image.",
  "seoDescription": "Rotate a 2D image around its center with nearest-neighbor sampling and defined out-of-bounds handling.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: image = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]], angle_degrees = 0\n\nOutput: [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]\n\nExplanation: A zero-degree inverse mapping selects every original pixel.",
    "Input: image = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]], angle_degrees = 180\n\nOutput: [[9, 8, 7], [6, 5, 4], [3, 2, 1]]"
  ],
  "requirements": "* Rotate around the image center, not the top-left corner\n* Use inverse mapping: for each output pixel, find the source pixel\n* Apply round() for nearest neighbor selection\n* Fill out-of-bounds pixels with 0",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Image Rotation (Nearest Neighbor)\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nImages possess rich spatial structure: nearby pixels are correlated, and visual patterns (edges, textures, object parts) are translation invariant. Computer vision models apply local receptive fields and weight sharing to process high-resolution visual inputs with minimal parameter overhead. **Image Rotation (Nearest Neighbor)** extracts localized visual features, pools spatial maps, or reconciles geometric bounding boxes.\n\nIn this problem, the objective is to implement **Image Rotation (Nearest Neighbor)** efficiently and accurately adhering to standard mathematical conventions.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe operation is governed by the following mathematical formulation:\n\n$$\nc_y = \\frac{H - 1}{2}\n$$\n\nEvery variable and index in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1: Input Validation & Pre-processing**: Validate tensor dimensions, convert inputs into contiguous array buffers, and extract structural shape parameters.\n2. **Phase 2: Core Transformation**: Apply vectorized operations directly across array dimensions without explicit Python loops.\n3. **Phase 3: Aggregation & Normalization**: Compute required summations, projections, or element-wise bounds while safeguarding against numerical instability.\n4. **Phase 4: Output Formatting**: Ensure the resulting array or dictionary matches expected output data types and precision tolerances.\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Receptive Field Growth**: Stacking convolutional layers with strides or pooling progressively expands the effective field of view, enabling deep layers to capture global semantic context.\n* **Boundary Padding & Strides**: Padding controls spatial dimension retention, while strides downsample feature resolutions efficiently without extra parameters.\n* **Vectorized im2col**: High-performance vision libraries transform sliding window convolutions into matrix multiplications (`GEMM`) via im2col operations.",
