  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nVectorized, numerically stable reference implementation following exact specifications.\n\n### 💻 Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport math\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef rotate_image(image: list, angle_degrees: float) -> list:\n    if angle_degrees == 0:\n        return image\n    arr = np.array(image)\n    H, W = arr.shape\n    rad = math.radians(angle_degrees)\n    cos_a, sin_a = math.cos(rad), math.sin(rad)\n    cy, cx = (H - 1) / 2.0, (W - 1) / 2.0\n    out = np.zeros_like(arr)\n    for y in range(H):\n        for x in range(W):\n            src_x = cos_a * (x - cx) + sin_a * (y - cy) + cx\n            src_y = -sin_a * (x - cx) + cos_a * (y - cy) + cy\n            nx, ny = int(round(src_x)), int(round(src_y))\n            if 0 <= nx < W and 0 <= ny < H:\n                out[y, x] = arr[ny, nx]\n    return out.tolist()\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the input dimensions.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ for memory buffers.\n",
